## Proof

We determine the point spectrum of $A$ by solving the eigenvalue problem
$$-f''(x) = \lambda f(x), \quad x \in \mathbb{R}_+,\qquad f'(0)+\beta f(0)=0,\qquad f\in L^2(\mathbb{R}_+),\ f\neq 0.$$

We analyze the three possible signs of $\lambda$.

**Case $\lambda > 0$.** Writing $\lambda = k^2$ with $k>0$, the general solution is
$$f(x)=A\cos(kx)+B\sin(kx).$$
These solutions are bounded but oscillatory; they do not decay and are not in $L^2(\mathbb{R}_+)$. Hence no positive $\lambda$ is an eigenvalue.

**Case $\lambda = 0$.** Then $f''=0$, so $f(x)=Ax+B$. For $f\in L^2(\mathbb{R}_+)$ we need $A=0$, giving $f\equiv B$, a constant. A nonzero constant is not square-integrable on $\mathbb{R}_+$, so $\lambda=0$ is not an eigenvalue.

**Case $\lambda < 0$.** Write $\lambda = -\mu^2$ with $\mu > 0$. The equation becomes $f''=\mu^2 f$, with general solution
$$f(x)=A e^{\mu x}+B e^{-\mu x}.$$
The requirement $f\in L^2(\mathbb{R}_+)$ forces $A=0$ (since $e^{\mu x}$ grows exponentially), leaving
$$f(x)=B e^{-\mu x},\qquad B\neq 0.$$
Applying the boundary condition $f'(0)+\beta f(0)=0$:
$$f'(0)=-\mu B,\qquad f(0)=B \implies -\mu B+\beta B = B(\beta-\mu)=0.$$
Since $B\neq 0$, we obtain $\mu = \beta$. As $\mu>0$, this is admissible **if and only if $\beta > 0$**.

When $\beta>0$, the unique eigenvalue is $\lambda = -\beta^2 < 0$ with eigenfunction $f(x)=e^{-\beta x}\in L^2(\mathbb{R}_+)$ (one readily checks $f'(0)+\beta f(0)=-\beta+\beta=0$).

When $\beta \leq 0$, the equation $\mu=\beta$ has no solution with $\mu>0$, so no negative eigenvalue exists; combined with the exclusion of $\lambda\geq 0$, the point spectrum is empty.

**Conclusion.** The operator $A$ has a (nonempty) point spectrum if and only if
$$\boxed{\beta > 0.}$$
In that case the point spectrum consists of the single eigenvalue $\lambda=-\beta^2$ with eigenfunction $e^{-\beta x}$.

